== lit session get --help
Read session and current properties.

Requirements:
  No required property flags. Without property flags, show every session property, including the logical session name
  and its distinct client UUID. --session-id selects only the client UUID; --stage selects only the workflow stage
  (null when none is set). Property selectors take no values.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.

Valid forms:
  lit session get [--session NAME] [--project] [--actor-name] [--status] [--stage]
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit session get [flags]

Examples:
  lit session get --session my-session

Flags:
      --actor-kind           Optional. Show session property actor-kind
      --actor-name           Optional. Show session property actor-name
  -h, --help                 help for get
      --output-format        Optional. Show session property output-format
      --project              Optional. Show session property project
      --revision             Optional. Show session property revision
      --runtime-session-id   Optional. Show session property runtime-session-id
      --runtime-vendor       Optional. Show session property runtime-vendor
      --service-id           Optional. Show session property service-id
      --session-id           Optional. Show session property session-id
      --stage                Optional. Show session property stage
      --status               Optional. Show session property status

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit session set --help
Set explicit session properties.

Requirements:
  Required: at least one property flag with a value.
  Properties: --project, --output-format, --actor-name, --actor-kind,
  --runtime-vendor, --runtime-session-id, --stage. Multiple properties may be set together.
  Stage: the workflow stage this session is running, stored per session on lit-server. Setting or clearing it creates no
  issue history, and it survives workflow-session resume. Any label of lowercase letters, digits and single hyphens
  (starting with a letter, at most 32 characters) is accepted; it is not checked against the standard stages (planner,
  clarify, research, prototype, to-spec, to-tickets, triage, impl, orchestrate), which completion suggests.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.

Valid forms:
  lit session set --project REF [--output-format cli|markdown|json]
  lit session set --actor-name NAME --actor-kind human|agent
  lit session set --stage STAGE
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit session set [flags]

Examples:
  lit session set --session my-session --project test/poc

Flags:
      --actor-kind KIND         Optional. Actor KIND: human or agent
      --actor-name NAME         Optional. Actor display NAME
  -h, --help                    help for set
      --output-format FORMAT    Optional individually; select at least one property. Session output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json
      --project REF             Optional individually; select at least one property. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection
      --runtime-session-id ID   Optional individually; select at least one property. Vendor runtime session ID
      --runtime-vendor NAME     Optional individually; select at least one property. Runtime vendor NAME
      --stage STAGE             Optional individually; select at least one property. Workflow STAGE of this session, for example impl or to-spec; see Stage in the requirements

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit session unset --help
Clear optional session properties.

Requirements:
  Required: at least one optional property selector (no value).
  Choose --project, --output-format, --runtime-vendor, --runtime-session-id or --stage.
  Actor name and kind cannot be unset. Unsetting --stage removes the workflow stage;
  the session then has none, as after connecting.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.

Valid forms:
  lit session unset --project [--output-format]
  lit session unset --runtime-vendor --runtime-session-id
  lit session unset --stage
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit session unset [flags]

Examples:
  lit session unset --session my-session --project

Flags:
      --actor-kind           Optional. Clear session property actor-kind
      --actor-name           Optional. Clear session property actor-name
  -h, --help                 help for unset
      --output-format        Optional individually; select at least one property. Clear session property output-format
      --project              Optional individually; select at least one property. Clear session property project
      --runtime-session-id   Optional individually; select at least one property. Clear session property runtime-session-id
      --runtime-vendor       Optional individually; select at least one property. Clear session property runtime-vendor
      --stage                Optional individually; select at least one property. Clear session property stage

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit projects --help
Create and discover project containers

Usage:
  lit projects [flags]
  lit projects [command]

Available Commands:
  create      Create projects atomically
  get         Read projects and current properties
  history     Read immutable mutation history
  list        List and filter projects
  update      Update projects atomically

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for projects

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)

Use "lit projects [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== lit projects create --help
Create projects atomically.
Repeat the item boundary flag to submit an atomic batch, or use --file with a JSON array.
Scalar fields may occur once per item; repeated set fields preserve input order.

Requirements:
  Required: --project-title TITLE for each flag-defined item, or --file PATH (nonempty JSON array).
  Do not mix --file with item flags. Repeat the boundary flag before each item's fields.
  Content is optional. --content and --content-file are mutually exclusive per item; stdin (-) may be read only once.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.

Valid forms:
  lit projects create --project-title TITLE [--content TEXT | --content-file PATH]
  lit projects create --project-title TITLE [ITEM_FLAGS] --project-title TITLE [ITEM_FLAGS]
  lit projects create --file PATH
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.
  FILTERS / ITEM_FLAGS / CHANGE_FLAGS refer to applicable flags below.

Usage:
  lit projects create [flags]

Examples:
  lit projects create --session my-session --project-title test/poc --content 'Try lit'

Flags:
      --content TEXT          Optional. UTF-8 content TEXT; conflicts with --content-file
      --content-file PATH     Optional. Read UTF-8 content from PATH; - reads stdin once
      --file PATH             Conditional (see valid forms). Input PATH: mutation JSON array or typed query object; - reads stdin once
  -h, --help                  help for create
      --project REF           Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection
      --project-title TITLE   Conditional (see valid forms). New project TITLE; repeat to begin another atomic item
      --repository REF        Optional. Repository REF associated with this project (repeatable)

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit projects get --help
Read projects and current properties.
With --format markdown, one complete returned record becomes YAML frontmatter
plus the exact original Markdown body. Duplicate selectors may return one record;
multiple returned records use a structured report. Export each separately for documents.
This is a read snapshot, not an import format: --content-file reads the entire file as body.

Requirements:
  Required: a project target, from REF, --project REF, or session selection.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.

Valid forms:
  lit projects get [REF...]
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit projects get [REF...] [flags]

Examples:
  lit projects get --session my-session test/poc

Flags:
  -h, --help          help for get
      --project REF   Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit projects list --help
List and filter projects.
Filters combine with AND; set filters may repeat. --all conflicts with --limit/--cursor.
Reuse the same filters with a returned cursor. --all does not widen project scope.

Requirements:
  No required query flags.
  Choose flag filters or a typed JSON query object: cannot combine --file with query/filter flags.
  --all conflicts with --limit/--cursor; --all does not widen project scope.
  Reuse the same filters with --cursor. Filters combine with AND.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.

Valid forms:
  lit projects list [FILTERS] [--limit N] [--cursor TOKEN]
  lit projects list [FILTERS] --all
  lit projects list --file PATH
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.
  FILTERS / ITEM_FLAGS / CHANGE_FLAGS refer to applicable flags below.

Usage:
  lit projects list [flags]

Examples:
  lit projects list --session my-session --limit 20

Flags:
      --all                      Optional. Return all results as one bounded snapshot; conflicts with --limit/--cursor
      --created-after RFC3339    Optional. Inclusive creation boundary RFC3339
      --created-before RFC3339   Optional. Exclusive creation boundary RFC3339
      --cursor TOKEN             Optional. Continue the same query with opaque TOKEN
      --direction DIRECTION      Optional. Sort DIRECTION: asc or desc
      --file PATH                Optional. Input PATH: mutation JSON array or typed query object; - reads stdin once
  -h, --help                     help for list
      --id UUID                  Optional. Match object UUID (repeatable)
      --limit N                  Optional. Page size N (default 100, maximum 1000 unless configured)
      --project REF              Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection
  -q, --query TEXT               Optional. Case-insensitive content substring TEXT
      --repository-ref REF       Optional. Match repository REF (repeatable)
      --sort FIELD               Optional. Sort FIELD: created-at, updated-at, title
      --title TITLE              Optional. Object TITLE (exact title filter on list)
      --updated-after RFC3339    Optional. Inclusive update boundary RFC3339
      --updated-before RFC3339   Optional. Exclusive update boundary RFC3339

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit projects update --help
Update projects atomically.
Repeat the item boundary flag to submit an atomic batch, or use --file with a JSON array.
Scalar fields may occur once per item; repeated set fields preserve input order.

Requirements:
  Required: target(s) using one of the forms below.
  Choose one input mode: positional targets, boundary-flag groups, or --file PATH (JSON array).
  Do not mix --file with item flags or positional targets; grouped and positional targets cannot mix.
  Use change flags to set, add, remove or clear fields; unchanged values are safe no-ops. --content and --content-file are mutually exclusive per item; stdin (-) may be read only once.
  Without explicit targets, update uses --project REF or session selection.
  Positional/selected-project mode needs a change flag or --revision to define an item.
  CHANGE_FLAGS are the set/add/remove/clear flags listed below (for example --content TEXT).
  Repeat the boundary flag before each item's fields; positional targets share one set of fields.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.

Valid forms:
  lit projects update REF... (CHANGE_FLAGS | --revision N)
  lit projects update --project-id REF [CHANGE_FLAGS] [--revision N] [--project-id REF [CHANGE_FLAGS] [--revision N]]
  lit projects update --file PATH
  lit projects update [--project REF] (CHANGE_FLAGS | --revision N)
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.
  FILTERS / ITEM_FLAGS / CHANGE_FLAGS refer to applicable flags below.

Usage:
  lit projects update [REF...] [flags]

Examples:
  lit projects update --session my-session test/poc --content 'Updated details'

Flags:
      --add-repository REF      Optional. Add repository REF (repeatable)
      --clear FIELD             Optional. Clear FIELD (repeatable): content, repositories
      --content TEXT            Optional. UTF-8 content TEXT; conflicts with --content-file
      --content-file PATH       Optional. Read UTF-8 content from PATH; - reads stdin once
      --file PATH               Conditional (see valid forms). Input PATH: mutation JSON array or typed query object; - reads stdin once
  -h, --help                    help for update
      --project REF             Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection
      --project-id REF          Conditional (see valid forms). Project REF; repeat to begin another update item
      --remove-repository REF   Optional. Remove repository REF (repeatable)
      --revision N              Optional. Expected object revision N; reject stale mutations
      --title TITLE             Optional. Object TITLE (exact title filter on list)

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
== lit projects history --help
Read immutable mutation history.
Use a reference (title or UUID) and pagination, or select one --request-hash.

Requirements:
  Required: a project target, from REF, --project REF, or session selection.
  --request-hash selects one entry; pagination flags are ignored in that mode.
  --all conflicts with --limit/--cursor.
  Required session: --session NAME or LIT_SESSION; connect that session first.

References:
  Project REF: exact title, UUID or unique UUID prefix.
  Use title:TITLE for literal title matching, or id:UUID_PREFIX for ID-only matching.

Valid forms:
  lit projects history [REF] [--limit N] [--cursor TOKEN]
  lit projects history [REF] --all
  lit projects history [REF] --request-hash HASH
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit projects history [REF] [flags]

Examples:
  lit projects history --session my-session test/poc

Flags:
      --all                 Optional. Return all results as one bounded snapshot; conflicts with --limit/--cursor
      --cursor TOKEN        Optional. Continue the same query with opaque TOKEN
  -h, --help                help for history
      --limit N             Optional. Page size N (default 100, maximum 1000 unless configured)
      --project REF         Optional. Project REF: title or UUID; otherwise session selection
      --request-hash HASH   Optional. Find history for transaction HASH

Global Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > remembered session > http://127.0.0.1:7411
      --format FORMAT      Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (otherwise session preference)
      --session NAME       Conditional. Logical session NAME; --session > LIT_SESSION; see requirements
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)
