Flags:
  -h, --help             help for checkout
      --path DIRECTORY   Required checkout DIRECTORY on this machine
      --repository REF   Required repository REF to associate with this checkout

Global Flags:
      --cli EXECUTABLE   Optional. Path to lit EXECUTABLE (default: this binary)
      --host HOST        Required agent HOST: codex or claude
      --runtime-id ID    Required explicit vendor runtime session ID
== lit hook --help
Serve the hooks an agent host (Claude Code or Codex) calls at session events. The host writes its hook JSON to stdin and
reads the output from stdout; hooks are installed into the host's configuration, not run by hand. Session details reach the
agent only through the hook output, never through environment variables. Once an event is being served the command exits 0
whatever happens, so a hook never breaks the host session; failures are written to the hook log in the workflow state
directory (hooks/hook.log). Argument errors exit 2 like other commands.

Usage:
  lit hook [flags]
  lit hook [command]

Available Commands:
  session-start  Hand the agent its lit session at session start
  statusline     Print the Claude status line, ending with lit's state
  stop           Remind the agent once about held claims before it stops
  subagent-start Give a subagent its own lit session

Flags:
  -h, --help        help for hook
      --host HOST   Required agent HOST: codex or claude

Use "lit hook [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== lit hook session-start --help
Serve the host's SessionStart hook. Read the hook JSON on stdin; create the lit session for a new runtime (startup, clear,
fork) or reconnect the known one (resume), keyed by the host session_id, and print host output whose additionalContext names
the session, endpoint, runtime ID, selected project and current claims. A server that does not answer creates nothing and is
reported in the context. After a compaction (source compact) print the session, project, the claims it holds with their lease
expiry and an excerpt of each claimed issue's latest comment, without writing anything to the tracker. The command exits 0
whatever happens; errors go to the hook log.

Requirements:
  Required flag: --host.
  The host's hook JSON is read from stdin and its output written to stdout.
  No tracker session is needed: the runtime ID is the hook input's session_id.
  Endpoint: LIT_ENDPOINT, otherwise http://127.0.0.1:7411, for a new runtime; a known runtime keeps its saved endpoint.
  Exits 0 whatever happens once the event is served; errors go to the hook log.

Valid forms:
  lit hook --host codex|claude session-start < hook-input.json
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit hook session-start [flags]

Examples:
  lit hook --host claude session-start < hook-input.json

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for session-start

Global Flags:
      --host HOST   Required agent HOST: codex or claude
== lit hook statusline --help
Serve Claude Code's status line command. Read the status line JSON on stdin and print one line: an original status line,
then lit's part, joined with " | ". Without --original the original is a built-in line, "MODEL EFFORT | DIRECTORY | GIT |
context: N%". With --original COMMAND it is that command's output: the command runs through sh -c with the same stdin, at most
one second and the width left over in COLUMNS; lit's part is printed alone when it fails.
lit's part is "🔥 lit | PROJECT | claims:N | STAGE" (the last part only while a stage is set), "⚫ not lit" when the server does
not answer and "lit: pending" while the session's association is pending. "[hooks degraded]" follows it after hook errors of
the last ten minutes. In a narrow terminal (Claude sets COLUMNS) lit's part shortens first, then the built-in line collapses
step by step. The server is asked every few seconds at most; in between, the line is answered from a cache in the workflow
state directory, within about 300 ms. Only the Claude host has a custom status line.

Requirements:
  Required flag: --host claude (Codex has no custom status line; it prints nothing).
  The host's status line JSON is read from stdin and the line is written to stdout; the host sets COLUMNS to the terminal width.
  No tracker session is needed: the runtime ID is the input's session_id.
  lit's state is answered from a cache under the workflow state directory (hooks/statusline) and refreshed every few seconds.
  Exits 0 whatever happens once the event is served; errors go to the hook log.

Valid forms:
  lit hook --host claude statusline [--original COMMAND] < status-line-input.json
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit hook statusline [flags]

Examples:
  lit hook --host claude statusline < hook-input.json

Flags:
  -h, --help               help for statusline
      --original COMMAND   Optional. Status line COMMAND to print first, run through sh -c with the same stdin and the width left in COLUMNS

Global Flags:
      --host HOST   Required agent HOST: codex or claude
== lit hook stop --help
Serve the host's Stop hook. Read the hook JSON on stdin; when this session holds claims and the stop is not already the
result of a stop hook (stop_hook_active is false), print a block decision whose reason names the claims and asks the agent to
record a checkpoint or handoff and release them, or to continue. The stop that follows is allowed. Without claims, without a
connected session, when the server does not answer, and on any internal error nothing is printed and the stop is allowed. The
guard only reads: it never releases, renews or closes anything. The command exits 0 whatever happens; errors go to the hook log.

Requirements:
  Required flag: --host.
  The host's hook JSON is read from stdin and its output written to stdout.
  No tracker session is needed: the runtime ID is the hook input's session_id.
  Endpoint: LIT_ENDPOINT, otherwise http://127.0.0.1:7411, for a new runtime; a known runtime keeps its saved endpoint.
  Exits 0 whatever happens once the event is served; errors go to the hook log.

Valid forms:
  lit hook --host codex|claude stop < hook-input.json
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit hook stop [flags]

Examples:
  lit hook --host claude stop < hook-input.json

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for stop

Global Flags:
      --host HOST   Required agent HOST: codex or claude
== lit hook subagent-start --help
Serve the host's SubagentStart hook. Read the hook JSON on stdin and register the subagent, keyed by the input's agent_id, as a child
of the conversation's lit session (the input's session_id): its own client, endpoint and selected project taken explicitly from the
parent, and no claims. Print host output whose additionalContext names the subagent's session, endpoint, runtime ID and project.
Running again for the same agent_id (a resumed subagent) registers nothing and prints the same text. A conversation without a
lit session gets no subagent session. The command exits 0 whatever happens; errors go to the hook log.

Requirements:
  Required flag: --host.
  The host's hook JSON is read from stdin and its output written to stdout.
  No tracker session is needed: the subagent's runtime ID is the hook input's agent_id and its parent is the input's session_id.
  Endpoint and project: those of the parent's lit session (workflow-session subagent --endpoint --project); LIT_ENDPOINT is not read.
  Exits 0 whatever happens once the event is served; errors go to the hook log.

Valid forms:
  lit hook --host codex|claude subagent-start < hook-input.json
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit hook subagent-start [flags]

Examples:
  lit hook --host claude subagent-start < hook-input.json

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for subagent-start

Global Flags:
      --host HOST   Required agent HOST: codex or claude
== lit context --help
Remember, show, validate or forget the project for the current checkout: a machine-local
preference recording which lit project, on which lit-server, you chose for one checkout.
It is project context only: no client identity, claim token or session name is stored,
so a new agent conversation still gets its own client.

A checkout is the Git worktree root containing the current directory: every
subdirectory shares it, linked worktrees and each nested repository are separate
checkouts, and symlinked paths resolve to the same physical checkout. Outside Git
the checkout is the exact current directory; parents are never searched. A moved
checkout has a new path and remembers nothing until the project is remembered again.

Records are versioned files in checkout-context/ below the workflow state directory
(LIT_WORKFLOW_STATE_DIR; otherwise ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/lit/local on Linux,
~/Library/Application Support/lit/local on macOS, %LOCALAPPDATA%\lit\local on Windows),
separate from session associations, checkout-location maps, pending requests and
output caches. Changes are atomic and serialized per checkout; the last one wins.

No command needs a logical session, and all ignore LIT_SESSION. show and clear are offline:
they never contact lit-server. Only set and show --validate do, with one read-only request
that carries no client identity, so no client, claim or session is created. The endpoint is
--endpoint, then LIT_ENDPOINT, then (validating) the saved endpoint, then http://127.0.0.1:7411.
A different address reaching the same service is accepted; a different service ID is not.

Usage:
  lit context [flags]
  lit context [command]

Examples:
  lit context set --project feat/tracker
  lit context show
  lit context show --validate
  lit context clear

Available Commands:
  clear       Forget the project remembered for this checkout
  set         Remember a project for this checkout
  show        Show the project remembered for this checkout

Flags:
      --format FORMAT   Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (default cli; no session preference applies)
  -h, --help            help for context

Use "lit context [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== lit context set --help
Remember a project for this checkout.
set asks lit-server, in one read-only request without a session or client, to resolve the project, then
saves its stable ID, its current title, the answering service's ID and the endpoint used, replacing an
earlier valid preference for this checkout. The title is display text; the ID is the identity.
REF is a title (case-insensitive), a UUID, a unique UUID prefix, or the title: and id: forms.
Only set remembers a project: reading a project, selecting one for a session or passing --project to
another command does not. Nothing is saved, and the earlier preference is preserved, when validation
fails; set never falls back to another project, endpoint or service.

Requirements:
  Required flag: --project REF. set needs no session and creates no client; it makes one request to lit-server.
  Endpoint: --endpoint > LIT_ENDPOINT > http://127.0.0.1:7411. The saved endpoint is not consulted: set chooses the service,
  and the service ID it saves is the one that answered.
  A damaged or unsupported record stops set before the service is asked (invalid_local_state); lit context clear removes it.
  A request is one GET to lit-server with no client identity: no client, claim, session or pending request is created.
  Failures exit nonzero and leave the preference untouched; --timeout bounds the wait (default 30s):
    not_found: no project has that title or ID (exit 1)
    ambiguous_reference: a UUID prefix names several projects (exit 1)
    service_unavailable: lit-server could not be reached in time (exit 1); lit context clear works without it
    incompatible_service, invalid_response: the service does not support project context or answered unusably (exit 1)
    invalid_endpoint: --endpoint or LIT_ENDPOINT is not an HTTP(S) origin (exit 2); an unusable --endpoint never falls back to another
    invalid_local_state: a damaged or unsupported record (exit 1); lit context clear removes it
  Archived projects are not reported yet: lit has no archive state, so every existing project is available (tracked in lit issue 0c14ae79).
  Output: --format cli (default), markdown or json; outcome applied, with the replaced preference as previous.

Valid forms:
  lit context set --project REF [--endpoint URL] [--timeout DURATION] [--format cli|markdown|json]
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit context set [flags]

Examples:
  lit context set --project feat/tracker
  lit context set --project feat/tracker --endpoint http://127.0.0.1:7411

Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL to resolve the project on; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > http://127.0.0.1:7411
  -h, --help               help for set
      --project REF        Required Project REF to remember: title, UUID or unique UUID prefix (also the title: and id: forms)
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION, for example 30s (default 30s)

Global Flags:
      --format FORMAT   Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (default cli; no session preference applies)
== lit context show --help
Show the project remembered for this checkout.
Without --validate, show is offline: it reads only the local record and reports the saved data as
not validated, since the project may since have been renamed, archived or removed. It works while lit-server
is down. Nothing remembered is a successful, explicit empty result.
With --validate, show also asks lit-server whether the remembered service and project still match
and reports the project's current identity and title. Neither form changes the preference: a
renamed project is reported as renamed and the saved title is not rewritten.

Requirements:
  Required flags: none. Without --validate no session, endpoint or service is needed; the command works offline and
  the saved preference is reported with validated: false.
  With --validate: --endpoint > LIT_ENDPOINT > the saved endpoint > http://127.0.0.1:7411 picks the service; the remembered
  service ID and project ID are checked in one request. --endpoint and --timeout apply only with --validate.
  A validated project reports validated: true and current (service, project, title and the endpoint used); a project
  renamed since it was saved reports renamed: true and the saved title is not rewritten.
  With nothing remembered, show (and show --validate, which makes no network request) succeeds with remembered: false and the
  message No project remembered for this checkout.
  A request is one GET to lit-server with no client identity: no client, claim, session or pending request is created.
  Failures exit nonzero and leave the preference untouched; --timeout bounds the wait (default 30s):
    wrong_service: the service that answered is not the remembered one (exit 1); lit context set --project REF remembers a project on it
    not_found: the remembered project no longer exists on the service (exit 1); lit context clear forgets it
    service_unavailable: lit-server could not be reached in time (exit 1); lit context clear works without it
    incompatible_service, invalid_response: the service does not support project context or answered unusably (exit 1)
    invalid_endpoint: --endpoint or LIT_ENDPOINT is not an HTTP(S) origin (exit 2); an unusable --endpoint never falls back to another
    invalid_local_state: a damaged or unsupported record (exit 1); lit context clear removes it
  Archived projects are not reported yet: lit has no archive state, so every existing project is available (tracked in lit issue 0c14ae79).
  Output: --format cli (default), markdown or json.

Valid forms:
  lit context show [--format cli|markdown|json]
  lit context show --validate [--endpoint URL] [--timeout DURATION] [--format cli|markdown|json]
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit context show [flags]

Examples:
  lit context show
  lit context show --format json
  lit context show --validate

Flags:
      --endpoint URL       Optional. Service URL to validate against, with --validate; flag > LIT_ENDPOINT > saved endpoint > http://127.0.0.1:7411
  -h, --help               help for show
      --timeout DURATION   Optional. Positive request DURATION with --validate, for example 30s (default 30s)
      --validate           Optional. Also check the saved project against lit-server (one request, no session); changes nothing

Global Flags:
      --format FORMAT   Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (default cli; no session preference applies)
== lit context clear --help
Forget the project remembered for this checkout.
clear removes only this checkout's preference. It does not delete projects, disconnect sessions,
release claims or touch pending requests, session associations or other workflow state.

Requirements:
  Required flags: none. No session, endpoint or service is needed; the command works offline.
  The command is idempotent: clearing when nothing is remembered succeeds with outcome already_satisfied and creates nothing.
  A damaged or unsupported record is removed too (removed: damaged_record), which is how to recover from invalid_local_state.
  A change to this checkout's preference by another invocation is awaited briefly; a lock held longer fails with context_locked (exit 3).
  Output: --format cli (default), markdown or json.

Valid forms:
  lit context clear [--format cli|markdown|json]
  [] means optional; | separates alternatives; ... means repeatable.

Usage:
  lit context clear [flags]

Examples:
  lit context clear

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for clear

Global Flags:
      --format FORMAT   Optional. Output FORMAT: cli, markdown or json (default cli; no session preference applies)
== lit completion --help
Generate the autocompletion script for lit for the specified shell: bash, fish or zsh.
See each sub-command's help for details on how to use the generated script.

Usage:
  lit completion [flags]
  lit completion [command]

Available Commands:
  bash        Generate the autocompletion script for bash
  fish        Generate the autocompletion script for fish
  zsh         Generate the autocompletion script for zsh

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for completion

Use "lit completion [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== lit completion bash --help
Generate the autocompletion script for bash (bash 4.4 or later).

To load completions in your current shell session:

	source <(lit completion bash)

To load completions for every new session, add that line to ~/.bashrc.

Usage:
  lit completion bash [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for bash
== lit completion fish --help
Generate the autocompletion script for fish (fish 3.4 or later).

To load completions in your current shell session:

	lit completion fish | source

To load completions for every new session, run once:

	lit completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/lit.fish

Usage:
  lit completion fish [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for fish
== lit completion zsh --help
Generate the autocompletion script for zsh (zsh 5.8 or later).

If shell completion is not already enabled in your environment, enable it once:

	autoload -U compinit; compinit

To load completions in your current shell session:

	source <(lit completion zsh)

To load completions for every new session, save the script as _lit in a directory on $fpath,
for example:

	lit completion zsh > "${fpath[1]}/_lit"

Start a new shell for this setup to take effect.

Usage:
  lit completion zsh [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for zsh
